**Clarify scope**: I would ask whether this is the in-app document library of Acrobat Desktop (folders, recent files, favourites) or low-level storage. I will assume the in-app library: users organise PDFs in folders, open/rename/move/delete them, search, and see metadata.

**Model (Composite pattern)**: `FileSystemNode { id, name, parent, createdAt, modifiedAt, permissions }` with two subclasses:
- `Folder { children: Map<name, node> }`
- `PdfFile { size, pageCount, storageRef, metadata (author, title, tags), version }`
Names are unique within a folder; the map gives `O(1)` lookup. A `FileSystem`/`Library` class holds `root` and offers: `createFolder(path)`, `addFile(path, file)`, `move(src, dstFolder)`, `rename`, `delete`, `list(path)`, `find(name)`, `getFile(path)`.

**Path resolution**: split on `/` and walk from the root; `O(depth)`. Moving checks that the target is not a descendant of the source (to avoid cycles) and that the name does not already exist.

**Separate metadata from content**: node objects hold the metadata, and the file bytes live in a storage layer addressed by `storageRef` (local disk or cloud). This lets rename and move be metadata-only operations, and lets the library sync with a cloud (Document Cloud) later.

**Extensions to mention**:
- search by name, tag or content via an index (a trie or an inverted index);
- recent files via an LRU list;
- delete as soft delete into a trash folder with restore;
- versioning and conflict handling for sync;
- permissions and sharing;
- a Visitor to compute folder sizes or export;
- an observer for UI updates when the tree changes;
- thread safety using a read-write lock because the UI and background sync access the tree.

Note complexity, error handling (name conflicts, missing paths), and how you would test it.
